#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# reference-transaction — layer 2 of the worktree location guard.
#
# NOT a Claude Code hook. Git invokes this itself, once per ref transaction, as a
# `reference-transaction` hook (githooks(5)). The contract was measured live in a throwaway
# repo rather than taken from memory — git 2.50.1 (Apple Git-155), ref-format `files`:
#
#   * argv is exactly one argument, the STAGE: `prepared`, `committed` or `aborted`.
#   * stdin carries the transaction's ref updates, one per line, as
#     `<old-value> SP <new-value> SP <ref-name> LF`. A single transaction may carry several.
#     A HEAD move reads `0000…0000 ref:refs/heads/other HEAD`; a detach writes an OID in the
#     same slot; an ordinary commit sends `<old> <new> refs/heads/<b>` and never HEAD at all.
#
# THE RULE, ENTIRE — four bails and one file test, in this order:
#
#   1. the stage is not `prepared`                        -> allow (git ignores a veto there)
#   2. no ref in this transaction is exactly `HEAD`        -> allow (not a HEAD move)
#   3. the ref backend is not `files`                      -> DENY (see "the backend" below)
#   4. --absolute-git-dir != --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir  -> allow (linked worktree)
#   5. <that common dir>/HEAD.lock exists                  -> DENY, else allow
#
# WHY THAT IS THE WHOLE RULE. `git worktree add` and `git switch` are byte-identical at the
# gated HEAD write — same ref, same new value, GIT_DIR unset in both. They are not identical
# in the FILESYSTEM: `switch`/`checkout`/`switch --detach` hold the primary's own HEAD.lock,
# while `worktree add` holds `worktrees/<n>/HEAD.lock` and never moves the primary's HEAD at
# all. So the lock is the discriminator, and the guard needs to recognise nothing about the
# command. There is deliberately no token, no nonce, no expiry, no ledger, and no command
# inspection here — an earlier design had all five and the lock rule deleted every one of
# them. Do not reintroduce them; the card records what each one failed to close.
#
# DENY means exit non-zero at `prepared`. Git turns that into rc=128 with the ref unmoved.
# There is no exit-2 convention here and no `worktree-guard:` prefix — those belong to
# layer 1, which is a PreToolUse hook and a different protocol. The prefix below is this
# hook's own, and is deliberately not a substring of layer 1's, so a refusal in a session's
# scrollback says which layer produced it.
#
# ⚠️ A VETO LEAVES THE DESTINATION BRANCH'S CONTENT STAGED. Stopping the ref write does not
# undo the checkout, and this hook cannot roll back from inside a veto. The message that fact
# requires — HEAD's unmoved branch, the staged destination, any sequencer directory by name,
# and the one exit that can be offered — is the refusal-remediation contract at step 5 below
# (task 6d). No message in this file prescribes a destructive command, and none describes the
# tree as clean, unchanged or restored.
#
# WORKTREE_EXEMPT is honoured, at that one refusal and nowhere else — it is the forward exit
# the message names, and a message naming an escape hatch the hook did not honour would be
# prescribing a command that fails. WORKTREE_GUARD_MODE is still deliberately absent, so
# nobody reads the absence as an oversight: layer 2's mode source is task 6e, which must not
# depend on an environment read this hook cannot verify reaches it.
#
# Design, boundaries (28-37) and scenarios: docs/features/worktree-location-guard.md.
#
# Fails CLOSED, like layer 1 and for the same reason: a guard that switches itself off
# exactly when it cannot verify its own precondition is indistinguishable from the feature
# being absent. Every "I could not evaluate this" below denies.

set -u

# Every probe below branches on a DIAGNOSTIC TEXT rather than an exit code, and `ls` renders
# its diagnostics in the caller's locale. Under a non-English LC_MESSAGES "No such file or
# directory" would never match, the absent-lock case would fall through to the unusable-probe
# deny, and the guard would refuse every HEAD move in the repo. Pinned rather than trusted.
export LC_ALL=C

LF='
'
# Spelled as a literal tab. Bash 3.2 is the floor here and $'\t' is not available in every
# position this value is used in; the test suite pins the separator the same way.
TAB='	'

# git rev-parse --path-format=absolute is what makes the scope test in step 4 discriminate at
# all, and it landed in git 2.31 — the same floor layer 1 established. Below the floor this
# hook denies rather than guessing.
#
# The floor check here is DIAGNOSTIC, not load-bearing, and that is worth saying plainly:
# unlike layer 1, every probe failure in this file already denies (boundaries 28-30), so a
# git too old to know the option lands on a deny either way. What the check buys is a message
# that names the real cause instead of "a rev-parse printed nothing".
FLOOR_MAJOR=2
FLOOR_MINOR=31
FLOOR='2.31'

# The one ref this rule is about. Compared for EQUALITY, never as a prefix or a substring:
# `refs/heads/HEAD` and `HEADS` both contain it and neither is it, and matching loosely would
# gate ordinary branch work in every repository on the machine.
GATED_REF='HEAD'

# The only ref backend whose lock this rule is defined for. An ALLOWLIST of one and not a
# denylist of `reftable`: a backend nobody has heard of yet must land on the same refusal.
SUPPORTED_BACKEND='files'

# The lockfile whose presence IS the rule, relative to the common git directory.
LOCK_NAME='HEAD.lock'

# The sequencer markers the refusal names when they are present, relative to the common git
# directory. Each one means an operation is mid-flight whose own `--abort` is the only thing
# that ends it, so a message describing the tree without naming it describes half the state.
# Measured 2026-08-26: a `git rebase` whose ref transaction is vetoed leaves `rebase-merge`
# here, and it is already present at the rebase's FIRST transaction.
SEQUENCER_NAMES='rebase-merge rebase-apply CHERRY_PICK_HEAD REVERT_HEAD MERGE_HEAD'

# The bypass, same name and same shape as layer 1's and as MERGE_EXEMPT/TEST_EXEMPT/
# JUDGE_EXEMPT. Layer 1 reads it off the COMMAND LINE, because a PreToolUse hook's own
# environment is the session's and not the command's; layer 2 has no command line at all and
# reads its own environment, which task 6c measured survives the `git` -> hook hop for both
# the assignment-prefix form and the inherited form.
EXEMPT_VAR='WORKTREE_EXEMPT'

# Same override knob as layer 1's, so one setting moves both logs. Task 6c measured that an
# inherited environment variable survives the git -> hook hop, which is what makes this
# reachable at all from here. `${HOME:-}` and not `$HOME` — under `set -u` an unset HOME is an
# unbound variable, and dying on it would turn a missing environment into a refused HEAD move
# for a reason that has nothing to do with the rule.
STATE_DIR="${WORKTREE_GUARD_STATE_DIR:-${HOME:-}/.claude/hooks/state}"
LOG_FILE="$STATE_DIR/reference-transaction.log"

# This hook's own prefix. NOT a superstring of layer 1's `worktree-guard:` — the two hooks
# can both refuse in one session and a shared token would make the log unsplittable.
PREFIX='worktree-location-guard (layer 2):'

# --- the attribution log ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Unlike layer 1's refusal-only log, this one records ALLOWS too, and that is a requirement
# rather than a preference: a run in which every case denied proves nothing about the allow
# clause, so the clause that decided each HEAD transaction has to be recoverable afterwards.
# The volume argument that made layer 1 refusal-only does not apply — nothing is written
# until a transaction has passed BOTH the stage bail and the HEAD-ref bail, and a HEAD write
# is a branch switch, not an every-command event.
#
# Format, tab-separated: <utc-timestamp> <DECISION clause> <detail>. Decision and clause share
# one field so the pair reads as one token both to a human tailing the file and to a
# `grep -xF` in the suite.
append_log() { # $1 "DECISION clause", $2 detail
  local ts detail
  ts=$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ') || return 1
  # A ref name cannot contain a newline, but a diagnostic quoted back into the detail field
  # can. A line-oriented log whose fields hold raw newlines is not parseable. The TAB escape
  # is the same argument one field over: the detail now carries a USER-SUPPLIED string (the
  # bypass reason), and a tab inside it would invent a fourth field in a three-field format.
  detail=${2//"$LF"/\\n}
  detail=${detail//"$TAB"/\\t}
  mkdir -p "$STATE_DIR" 2>/dev/null
  # The append runs in a subshell so bash's own "cannot create" diagnostic for a failed
  # redirection lands there and not in the middle of git's output. A trailing `2>/dev/null`
  # would not do it: redirections are applied left to right, so the failure is reported
  # before the suppression is in place.
  ( printf '%s%s%s%s%s\n' "$ts" "$TAB" "$1" "$TAB" "$detail" >>"$LOG_FILE" ) 2>/dev/null
}

# --- the two exits ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A failed append never changes the verdict, in either direction. Turning an allow into a deny
# because a disk filled up would refuse every HEAD move on the machine for a reason unrelated
# to the rule; turning a deny into an allow would switch the guard off at exactly the moment
# it could not record why. The lost line is announced on stderr instead, which is its only
# trace — the same argument layer 1's MSG_BYPASS_WARN makes about its own one-way loss.
MSG_LOG_WARN="$PREFIX this decision could not be appended to $LOG_FILE, so no record of it survives."

allow() { # $1 clause, $2 detail
  append_log "ALLOW $1" "$2" || printf '%s\n' "$MSG_LOG_WARN" 1>&2
  exit 0
}

deny() { # $1 clause, $2 detail, $3 message ('' for the silent denies)
  append_log "DENY $1" "$2" || printf '%s\n' "$MSG_LOG_WARN" 1>&2
  [ -n "$3" ] && printf '%s\n' "$3" 1>&2
  exit 1
}

# --- step 1: the stage --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ONLY `prepared` CAN VETO. A non-zero exit at `aborted` or `committed` is IGNORED by git —
# rc=0 and the ref moves anyway, measured — so judging at those stages blocks nothing extra;
# it prints a refusal for a move that then succeeded, a message contradicting the tree, which
# is worse than silence. The stage guard is load-bearing, not defensive tidiness.
#
# An UNRECOGNISED stage is the different case and takes the policy default: it may be one that
# CAN veto, so it denies. With no message — announcing a refusal that may be ineffective
# teaches the reader to distrust the effective ones. Boundary 35. Nothing beyond the three
# documented stages has ever been observed.
case "${1-}" in
  prepared) ;;
  committed|aborted) exit 0 ;;
  *) deny 'unrecognised-stage' "stage=[${1-<absent>}]" '' ;;
esac

# --- step 2: is this transaction about HEAD? ----------------------------------------------
#
# Boundary 32: the ref name is the only field that decides whether the transaction touches
# HEAD, so a line that does not split into exactly three fields is an undecidable transaction
# — and because one transaction may carry several lines (`git rebase` writes HEAD, then
# refs/heads/<b>, then HEAD), ONE bad line makes the whole transaction unclassifiable rather
# than just that ref. The loop therefore denies on the spot instead of skipping the line.
#
# Read STRAIGHT from stdin rather than through `stdin_text=$(cat)` first. Command substitution
# strips every trailing newline, so a transaction ending in a blank line would arrive here
# indistinguishable from one that did not — and that blank line is exactly the empty-line case
# boundary 32 denies. Reading the descriptor directly also keeps the loop in THIS shell: a
# `printf … | while` would put it in a subshell, where the `exit 1` inside deny() would end the
# subshell and let the hook fall through to an allow (hooks/README.md records that trap).
#
# The `|| [ -n "$tx_line" ]` is what keeps a final line with no trailing newline from being
# dropped: `read` returns non-zero at EOF while still having filled the variable.
saw_gated_ref=0
tx_count=0
# The destination the refusal names, taken from the transaction git sent and from nowhere
# else — this hook inspects no command line. `git switch <b>` sends `ref:refs/heads/<b>` in
# the new-value field; `git switch --detach` sends a bare OID there. A single transaction may
# carry more than one HEAD line, so every one is collected rather than the first being
# presented as the destination. Newline-separated: a ref value cannot contain one.
gated_targets=''
while IFS= read -r tx_line || [ -n "$tx_line" ]; do
  tx_count=$((tx_count+1))
  # Deliberate word splitting: the fields are separated by single spaces and none of the
  # three can contain one. `set --` is what makes the arity checkable, as $#.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  set -- $tx_line
  if [ "$#" -ne 3 ]; then
    deny 'unparseable-transaction-line' "fields=$# line=$tx_count" \
      "$PREFIX blocked — line $tx_count of this ref transaction did not split into the three
fields the reference-transaction contract specifies (<old-value> <new-value> <ref-name>); it
had $#. The ref name is what decides whether this transaction touches HEAD, so a line that
cannot be read makes the whole transaction undecidable."
  fi
  if [ "$3" = "$GATED_REF" ]; then
    saw_gated_ref=1
    gated_targets="$gated_targets$2$LF"
  fi
done

# Zero lines denies for the same reason (boundary 37): a descriptor that closed before any
# line arrived is indistinguishable from a transaction whose contents were lost, and at
# `prepared` a deny is still effective.
if [ "$tx_count" -eq 0 ]; then
  deny 'empty-transaction' 'no lines on stdin' \
    "$PREFIX blocked — the ref transaction arrived with no lines at all, so which refs it
touches is unknown. An unclassifiable transaction is not one this hook can permit."
fi

# Not a HEAD transaction. An ordinary commit lands here — it writes refs/heads/<b> and never
# HEAD — and committing in a primary checkout is not what this guard exists to stop. Nothing
# is logged: no clause was reached, and a line claiming otherwise would inflate the log with
# every commit in every repository on the machine.
[ "$saw_gated_ref" = 1 ] || exit 0

# --- step 3: git, its version, and the ref backend ----------------------------------------
#
# Boundary 36: every probe below is a `git` call, so an uninvocable git leaves the backend,
# the scope and the lock path all undetermined. Precedent: test-marker-guard's MSG_NO_PYTHON,
# which blocks everywhere.
if ! command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  deny 'no-git' 'command -v git failed' \
    "$PREFIX blocked — no git on PATH, so this hook could not read the ref backend, could not
tell a primary checkout from a linked worktree, and could not locate the lock its rule is
about. None of the three can be assumed."
fi

deny_version() { # $1 the raw --version output, quoted back so the failure is diagnosable
  deny 'git-below-floor' "$1" \
    "$PREFIX blocked — git is older than $FLOOR, or its version could not be read.

The scope test uses \`git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir\`, which requires
git >= $FLOOR. Without it that probe answers a path relative to the cwd, which never equals
--absolute-git-dir, so this hook would bail on every invocation and allow every HEAD move.

  git --version said: $1

Install git >= $FLOOR."
}

git_version=$(git --version 2>/dev/null) || deny_version '<git --version failed>'
version=${git_version#git version }
major=${version%%.*}
tail_version=${version#*.}
minor=${tail_version%%.*}
case "$major" in ''|*[!0-9]*) deny_version "$git_version" ;; esac
case "$minor" in ''|*[!0-9]*) deny_version "$git_version" ;; esac
if [ "$major" -lt "$FLOOR_MAJOR" ] ||
   { [ "$major" -eq "$FLOOR_MAJOR" ] && [ "$minor" -lt "$FLOOR_MINOR" ]; }; then
  deny_version "$git_version"
fi

# THE BACKEND, AND WHY IT IS CHECKED BEFORE THE RULE RATHER THAN NOT AT ALL. Boundary 28.
# Under `--ref-format=reftable` there is no <common-dir>/HEAD.lock, so step 5's deny clause
# can NEVER fire and the rule allows every HEAD write — a fail-open across an entire BACKEND
# rather than a missed shape. Refusing to arm converts a silent total bypass into a loud
# refusal to run, which is the trade this design takes everywhere else.
#
# ⬜ The git version at which --show-ref-format appeared is NOT measured — the only git
# available to this work is 2.50.1, which has it. A git that rejects the option exits
# non-zero and lands on this same deny, so the gap costs a worse message, not a wrong verdict.
ref_format=$(git rev-parse --show-ref-format 2>/dev/null) || ref_format=''
if [ "$ref_format" != "$SUPPORTED_BACKEND" ]; then
  deny 'backend-not-files' "read=[$ref_format]" \
    "$PREFIX refusing to run — this repository's ref backend reads as [${ref_format:-<no answer>}],
which this guard does not implement. It implements only [$SUPPORTED_BACKEND].

Its whole rule is the presence of <common-git-dir>/$LOCK_NAME. On any other backend that file
does not exist, so the refusal clause could never fire and every HEAD write would be allowed
— the guard would be silently off for this entire repository rather than for one command
shape. It refuses to run instead.

Nothing here is a judgement about the HEAD write you asked for. This guard cannot form one on
this backend, and it says so rather than passing everything through in silence."
fi

# --- step 4: is this the primary context? -------------------------------------------------
#
# --path-format=absolute IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT TIDINESS, and the failure it prevents is total.
# Measured: git chdirs a hook to the repository toplevel, so a bare `--git-common-dir` answers
# `.git` there while `--absolute-git-dir` answers /…/repo/.git. Those are NEVER equal — in the
# primary checkout or anywhere else — so a hook comparing the bare pair bails on every
# invocation and allows every HEAD move, while a liveness check goes on reporting it armed.
# That is precisely the failure this design names elsewhere: a guard whose absence is
# indistinguishable from its success.
#
# Boundaries 29 and 30: either probe exiting non-zero or printing nothing DENIES. Allowing
# would be the worse guess in both directions — the two paths would differ, which reads as
# "linked worktree", and an empty common dir is additionally the BASE of step 5's lock path,
# where it would test /HEAD.lock and silently stop the refusal clause from ever firing.
deny_probe() { # $1 the probe, $2 what it did
  deny 'probe-unusable' "$1 $2" \
    "$PREFIX blocked — \`git rev-parse $1\` $2, so this hook cannot tell whether this HEAD
write belongs to a shared primary checkout or to a linked worktree of its own.

A missing answer makes the two paths differ, which reads as 'linked worktree' and would
allow. An unusable probe refuses instead of guessing."
}

abs_git_dir=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) ||
  deny_probe '--absolute-git-dir' 'exited non-zero'
[ -n "$abs_git_dir" ] || deny_probe '--absolute-git-dir' 'exited 0 but printed nothing'

# The common directory is read HERE and used for the lock path below. It is never taken from
# $GIT_COMMON_DIR: GIT_DIR was measured UNSET in this hook's environment at the gated HEAD
# write, so its sibling cannot be relied on either, and the `$GIT_COMMON_DIR/HEAD.lock`
# notation in the design is a path expression, not an environment read (boundary 33).
common_dir=$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null) ||
  deny_probe '--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir' 'exited non-zero'
[ -n "$common_dir" ] ||
  deny_probe '--path-format=absolute --git-common-dir' 'exited 0 but printed nothing'

# The scope bail. A linked worktree's HEAD is its own and nobody else shares it, so it is
# none of this guard's business — and the hooks directory is shared with every linked
# worktree ($GIT_COMMON_DIR/hooks, measured), so without this test the guard would judge
# them all. Logged, because "the scope bail decided this" and "the allow clause decided this"
# are different facts and the suite has to be able to tell them apart.
[ "$abs_git_dir" = "$common_dir" ] || allow 'scope-not-primary' "$abs_git_dir"

# --- step 5: the lock ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `switch`, `checkout` and `switch --detach` hold this file; `git worktree add` holds
# worktrees/<n>/HEAD.lock and leaves the primary's HEAD where it was. That single fact is the
# whole discriminator, and it is why this hook inspects no command line.
#
# Boundary 31: "absent" is the rule's ALLOW answer, so every other outcome must be
# distinguished from it. A bare `[ -e ]` collapses "the file is not there" and "the test could
# not be performed" into the same false, which converts every EACCES, ELOOP or ENOTDIR into an
# allowed HEAD move. `ls -ld` separates them, branching on the diagnostic TEXT the way layer 1
# reads --show-toplevel — an exit code alone cannot, because `ls` reports every one of those
# as 1. LC_ALL=C at the top of this file is what makes the text stable enough to match.
lock_path="${common_dir%/}/$LOCK_NAME"
lock_diag=$(ls -ld -- "$lock_path" 2>&1 >/dev/null)
lock_rc=$?

if [ "$lock_rc" -eq 0 ]; then
  # THE REFUSAL, AND THE REMEDIATION CONTRACT THAT GOES WITH IT (task 6d, decided 2026-08-25
  # after the remedy this file used to point at was actually run: `git reset --hard HEAD`
  # destroyed another session's staged `other-session-work.txt`). Three rules hold whatever
  # else changes here, and the suite asserts each on the ABSENCE so a later revision cannot
  # quietly undo them:
  #
  #   * it prescribes no command that discards anything;
  #   * it never describes this tree as clean, unchanged or restored;
  #   * it offers no rollback exit, and says outright that it names no command for one.
  #
  # THE SECOND EXIT IS NOT MISSING BY OVERSIGHT. The card's contract reads "rollback only when
  # layer 1 recorded a clean pre-command tree". Layer 1 records no such thing — it runs no
  # pre-command `git status --porcelain` and writes no tree-state fact anywhere — and the
  # design section declines the cross-process handoff that would carry one for v1. So the
  # precondition is never satisfied and the exit is never offered; stating the precondition
  # and naming no command is exactly what the design prescribes until the handoff exists.
  # GROUP M's M10 is the tripwire: it asserts this message is byte-identical over a clean and
  # a dirty pre-command tree, so adding the handoff fails a test rather than passing one.

  # EXIT A, read lazily — only when a refusal is actually due, so an allowed HEAD write never
  # pays for the read and never logs a bypass it did not need. An EMPTY value is not a reason:
  # an accidental `WORKTREE_EXEMPT=` would otherwise be a silent machine-wide off switch.
  #
  # Scoped to THIS refusal and to no other. The backend refusal (boundary 28) is this guard
  # declining to run at all rather than a verdict about a HEAD write, and widening the hatch
  # to cover it is a decision the card has not made.
  #
  # A failed append must not turn the bypass into a refusal — the user reaching for the escape
  # hatch is by construction already blocked on something. allow() already has that shape: it
  # warns on stderr and exits 0. Layer 1 needs a bypass-specific warning because its own
  # MSG_LOG_WARN says "the guard is in log mode", which would be false here; this file's
  # MSG_LOG_WARN says only that the record is lost, which is true of a bypass too.
  if [ -n "${WORKTREE_EXEMPT:-}" ]; then
    allow 'bypass-worktree-exempt' "reason=[${WORKTREE_EXEMPT}]"
  fi

  # HEAD's own branch, READ rather than assumed. At `prepared` the ref has not moved, so this
  # is the branch the user is still on. A detached HEAD has no symbolic ref: it is reported as
  # what it is instead of a branch name being invented, which is the failure this card refuses
  # by name elsewhere. Neither read can change the verdict — both run inside a deny that has
  # already been decided — so a failure costs a worse line, not a wrong answer.
  head_now=$(git symbolic-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null) || head_now=''
  if [ -z "$head_now" ]; then
    head_oid=$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null) || head_oid=''
    if [ -n "$head_oid" ]; then
      head_now="<detached, at $head_oid>"
    else
      head_now='<could not be read: neither symbolic-ref nor rev-parse answered>'
    fi
  fi

  # Deliberate word splitting on both loops: neither a ref value nor a name in
  # $SEQUENCER_NAMES can contain whitespace.
  dest_block=''
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  for one_target in $gated_targets; do
    dest_block="$dest_block  destination:  $one_target$LF"
  done

  # `[ -e ]` here, and NOT boundary 31's `ls -ld`, on purpose: this text is DESCRIPTIVE and
  # never a verdict, so an unreadable path costs an omitted line rather than a wrong allow.
  # That asymmetry is also why nothing is printed when none is found — "no sequencer operation
  # is in progress" would be a claim this test cannot source.
  seq_block=''
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  for one_seq in $SEQUENCER_NAMES; do
    if [ -e "${common_dir%/}/$one_seq" ]; then
      seq_block="$seq_block  in progress:  $one_seq$LF"
    fi
  done

  deny 'primary-HEAD-lock-held' "$lock_path" \
    "$PREFIX HEAD write refused — this is the PRIMARY checkout of a shared repository.

Primary checkouts are shared by parallel sessions, which share their single HEAD: a branch
switch under another session's feet swaps the files it is editing, and nothing in the tree
records that it happened. \`git worktree add\` is unaffected — it holds a lock of its own and
never moves this HEAD — so creating a worktree and working there is not blocked.

  git dir:      $common_dir
  lock held:    $lock_path
  HEAD still:   $head_now
$dest_block$seq_block
WHAT THIS TREE HOLDS NOW. HEAD did not move, and the checkout was NOT undone: this hook stops
the ref write only, and git had already written the index and the working tree by the time it
ran. So the files here are the DESTINATION's content, staged, sitting on the branch HEAD still
names. \`git status\` shows that as ordinary staged work and carries no field saying where it
came from — a commit made now would land the destination's content on the branch above.

THE ONE EXIT THIS HOOK CAN OFFER IS FORWARD. Re-run the same command with
$EXEMPT_VAR=<reason> set; this hook reads that from its own environment and allows the write.
The tree already holds the destination's content, so letting the switch finish leaves this
checkout self-consistent instead of mixed. The cost is real and is stated rather than buried:
that is the guard overridden, not the guard satisfied, and the shared-HEAD problem above is
then live for every session working in this checkout.

GOING BACK IS YOURS TO DECIDE, AND THIS HOOK NAMES NO COMMAND FOR IT. From inside a veto it
sees the ref update and nothing else, so it cannot tell its own residue from work another
session staged — the two carry identical \`git status\` markers. Discarding here is safe only
if this checkout held nothing of its own before the command ran, and layer 2 has no record of
that: the layer that could take it does not (declined for v1). The precondition is stated
because it cannot be checked here. A blanket recovery command was tried during this guard's
own design and destroyed another session's staged file."
fi

case "$lock_diag" in
  # The one outcome that is an allow. `git worktree add` and every non-HEAD-moving operation
  # in the primary checkout land here.
  *'No such file or directory'*) allow 'no-primary-HEAD-lock' "$lock_path" ;;
esac

# Every other diagnostic: the existence test could not be performed. Denying is the only
# answer that does not read a permission error as permission. The diagnostic is quoted back
# because this branch refuses every HEAD move in the repository, and a deny that does not name
# what it read is indistinguishable from a deny for any other reason.
deny 'lock-probe-unusable' "$lock_diag" \
  "$PREFIX blocked — the test for $lock_path could not be performed.

  the system said: $lock_diag

'The file is absent' is this guard's ALLOW answer, so an error that is not absence cannot be
treated as one — doing so would turn every permission or symlink failure into an allowed HEAD
move in a shared checkout."
